I’ll first characterize the pattern and chemical context, then inspect the frozen analysis tooling and reference index. I’ll build a complete GSAS-II multiphase model early, preserve physical checkpoints, and only investigate residual peak families that remain coherent.

The specimen is a low-temperature MnO₂ + WO₃ synthesis, so the first physically ordinary inventory is residual MnO₂/WO₃ plus any manganese tungstate product or low-temperature manganese oxide. I’m now locating the provided indexing/refinement interfaces and extracting peak families before choosing reference structures.

The strongest observed peaks already separate into two familiar families after a common positive angular shift: monoclinic WO₃ gives the 23.44/23.93/24.69° triplet, while rutile-type MnO₂ explains 29.01, 37.67, and 56.96°. I’m comparing every exact-composition frozen reference now, because the correct polymorph and coordinate set matter more than the phase labels alone.

I’m moving from stick-pattern identification to a replayable GSAS-II model. The first checkpoint will contain both ordinary precursor phases with the correct Cu Kα doublet, Poisson weights, Bragg–Brentano geometry, and a smooth background; then I’ll inspect the residual before admitting any product phase.

The two-phase project is running, but full-grid GSAS-II refinement is slower than the stick screening. I’m preserving each stage so that any unstable profile or coordinate term can be rolled back; the current comparison is still limited to physically accepted WO₃ settings and rutile MnO₂.

The model has converged to roughly 68 wt% WO₃ and 32 wt% MnO₂ before coordinate/texture corrections, with Rwp about 17% at the cell-only checkpoint. Zero and specimen displacement are nearly collinear, so I’m keeping that calibration simple until the phase profile is better constrained.

No residual reflection family supports MnWO₄ or Mn₃WO₆: their diagnostic low-angle/product peaks are absent, and the remaining misfit alternates positive/negative across the already indexed WO₃/MnO₂ peaks. I’m stopping phase search and refining only those two accepted phases, with explicit bounds and geometry checks.

The bounded profile model remains physical, though its common Gaussian width reaches the allowed ceiling; WO₃ broadening is then represented by a ~0.20 µm domain size and ~3,100 ppm isotropic strain, while MnO₂ is effectively instrument-limited. I’m now testing bounded atomic-coordinate refinement, where the current fit is intensity-limited rather than phase-inventory-limited.

The first bounded coordinate pass stalled numerically before completing a cycle, so I stopped it without losing the physical stage-06 checkpoint. I’m treating that as an unknown refinement failure—not evidence against the structure—and isolating the coordinate variables in a one-cycle diagnostic before proceeding.

The bounded coordinates preserve octahedral connectivity and reasonable bond lengths, but several oxygen coordinates sit at their conservative limits and Rwp remains 6.47%. That pattern points to specimen intensity effects rather than another phase, so I’m testing one March–Dollase axis at a time for each accepted phase before adding any more structural freedom.

The two-phase model is now physically bounded at about 70.4 wt% monoclinic WO₃ and 29.6 wt% pyrolusite MnO₂, with every coherent reflection family assigned. I’m finishing the supported texture/profile checks, then I’ll lock the best full-grid checkpoint and export the CIFs, fit profile, JSON, report, and replayable GSAS-II project.

The texture scan found no meaningful WO₃ orientation effect. MnO₂ shows a modest but statistically clear, physically plausible (110) preference (March coefficient ≈0.895), improving Rwp from 6.471% to 6.426%. I’ll retain that single supported term and now test only whether a simple specimen-surface correction is warranted before the final coupled refinement.

The surface-roughness trial is not identifiable: its two parameters correlate at 100% and converge to different equivalent pairs for only a 0.014-point Rwp change. I’m rejecting that correction and preserving the simpler texture-only model. Before the final coupled pass, I’m checking the remaining residuals by angle and reflection family to ensure no justified profile term is being missed.

The residual check confirms the remaining large misfits are intensity/shape differences at already indexed WO₃ and MnO₂ peaks, not unassigned peaks. A wider but still chemically bounded coordinate refinement improves Rwp to 6.389% while respecting symmetry; the first branch completed, and I’m rerunning the remaining alternate starts after correcting a bookkeeping-only script error.

All three bounded coordinate starts converge to the same physical basin; the best is the continuation from the prior checkpoint (Rwp 6.389%, 71.79/28.21 wt%), while independent source and perturbed starts land within 0.005 Rwp points. I’m validating W–O/Mn–O geometry now; if it passes, I’ll use this reproducible basin and avoid adding unstable anisotropic broadening.

A single extra profile term is supported: WO₃ uniaxial microstrain along b gives 5,949 ppm transverse and 5,466 ppm axial strain, both positive and modestly anisotropic, lowering Rwp to 6.382%. Other axes collapse toward isotropy. I’m retaining this physically interpretable term and proceeding to the final full-grid coupled refinement; no further profile complexity is justified.

The full-grid refinement converged cleanly with no frozen parameters: Rwp 6.374%, Rp 4.617%, GOF 2.371, and crystalline fractions 71.79(22) wt% WO₃ / 28.21(22) wt% MnO₂ (formal 1σ). I’m now performing independent consistency checks on the exported CIF geometry, exact profile grid, fraction normalization, and GSAS-II replay before writing the uncertainty report.

All independent checks pass: the exported profile exactly matches the 8301-point input grid, calculated intensities are finite/nonnegative, fractions sum to 1, both CIFs parse with full occupancies and sixfold oxide coordination, and a cloned `final.gpx` refinement reproduces the submitted Rwp with no frozen parameters. The report now records the phase evidence and the limits on coordinate, fraction, amorphous, and trace-phase certainty.

Analysis complete. The crystalline specimen is:

- Monoclinic WO₃ (*P*2₁/*c*): 71.79 wt%
- Pyrolusite MnO₂ (*P*4₂/*mnm*): 28.21 wt%

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. No crystalline tungstate product was detected. Final fit: Rwp 6.374%, Rp 4.617%, GOF 2.371.

